// specialize: the head's instance at these args is ANTECEDENT-FREE, and the claim is a
// different formula entirely. Must fail in BOTH modes.
//
// This is the sharpest shape for the claim-vs-instance check: `pAll(ZERO)` instantiates to
// `p(ZERO)` with no `->` chain, so there is no antecedent a peel could mistake for a guard
// and no way to reach the claim by stripping. The only question is whether the instance's
// conclusion IS the claim — and it is not.
//
// Under `--fast` a `specialize` is admitted without proving the instance, which is fine: the
// head is trusted on its STATEMENT. What is NOT fine is admitting a claim the head cannot
// yield at those args, so the α-match must run in admit mode too.
sort Nat
const ZERO: Nat
pred p(n: Nat)
pred q(n: Nat)

axiom pAll: forall x: Nat; p(x)

// `pAll(ZERO)` yields `p(ZERO)`; this claims `q(ZERO)`, which it does not yield.
theorem unrelated: q(ZERO)
proof
  @conclusion |
    q(ZERO)
    [using specialize pAll(ZERO)]
qed
